<div class="<%= layout_framework_classes %>">
  <div class="<%= layout_framework_title_classes %>">
    <%= render 'framework/page_header', page_name: :sass_build %>
  </div>

  <div class="<%= layout_framework_content_classes %>">
    <%= render_framework_intro_paragraph(:sass_build) %>

    <div class="space-y-16">
      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Toolchain') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The framework compiles with Dart Sass; the module system rules out LibSass and node-sass. Releases pin
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">sass-embedded</code> 1.93.3 so published artifacts
            reproduce byte for byte, and any current Dart Sass produces equivalent CSS.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Compiling plugins.css') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Create an entrypoint that loads the framework module, then compile it with the framework source on the
            load path. <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">bin/build</code> in the repository runs
            exactly this for the released artifacts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(demo_code: '// my-build.scss
@use \'framework\';', language: 'scss') %>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(demo_code: 'sass --style=compressed --no-source-map \
  --load-path=trmnl-framework/app/assets/stylesheets \
  my-build.scss plugins.css', language: 'bash') %>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-4">
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            To bake your own device profiles into the build, configure the module at the same
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">@use</code> site. See
            <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :sass_devices) %>.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The compiled stylesheet embeds every dither tile as data URIs, so it is large on disk. Serve it
            compressed; the release pipeline ships a <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">.gz</code>
            next to every artifact for the same reason.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Fonts') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Every <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">@font-face</code> block in the framework points
            at a root-relative <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">/fonts/</code> URL, so a host has to
            serve those files. Without them the pixel families fall back to the browser default. Nothing errors, the
            type is simply wrong at every size.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Font URLs resolve against the host that serves the stylesheet, not the page. Link
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">plugins.css</code> from the releases CDN and the fonts
            load from the same host. Serve your own build and you serve
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">/fonts</code> with it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(4, 'What the host serves') %>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNL12</code>,
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNL16</code>, and
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNL21</code> in Regular and Bold, each as
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">.woff2</code>,
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">.woff</code>, and
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">.ttf</code> (18 files).
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">NicoPups-Regular.ttf</code>,
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">NicoClean-Regular.ttf</code>, and
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">BlockKie.ttf</code> for the Classic bundle (3 files).
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">Inter.ttf</code> and
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">Inter-Italic.ttf</code>, which both bundles use on
              high-density displays (2 files).
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Both bundles download as zips from
            <%= link_to 'Releases', framework_releases_index_path, class: 'font-medium hover:underline' %>, each with a
            README of per-font credits and its license text. Extract the font files into the directory the host serves
            at <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">/fonts</code>, and keep the credits and license
            files with your build. <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :font_family) %> covers which bundle renders what.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="<%= message_warning_classes %>">
          <div class="flex items-center w-full">
            <div class="flex-shrink-0 items-center">
              <%= render 'shared/icons/warning' %>
            </div>
            <div class="w-full flex items-center justify-between ml-3">
              <p class="leading-none text-sm font-medium">
                The framework release zip holds no fonts. They ship as their own bundles, so a stack assembled from
                that zip alone renders with no pixel fonts at all.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Palette tiles') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The compiled stylesheet embeds every dither tile as a data URI, on grayscale screens and color palettes
            alike. Fonts are the only asset it loads by URL, so a custom stack has no pattern images to serve.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Themes') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Themes are not bundled into <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">plugins.css</code>. Each
            theme under <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">framework/themes/</code> compiles to its
            own stylesheet and loads as a second <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">&lt;link&gt;</code>
            next to the framework. Usage is on <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :themes) %>; the authoring contract is
            on <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :theme_authoring) %>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(demo_code: 'sass --style=compressed --no-source-map \
  --load-path=trmnl-framework/app/assets/stylesheets \
  trmnl-framework/app/assets/stylesheets/framework/themes/black-and-yellow-theme.scss \
  themes/black-and-yellow-theme.css', language: 'bash') %>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'The JavaScript runtime') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">plugins.js</code> is hand-authored and ships verbatim:
            no bundler, no build step. Copy
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">app/javascript/plugin-render/plugins.js</code> next to
            your compiled CSS and serve both. It provides the render-time engines
            (<%= render DocsRef.new(page: :framework_runtime) %>) and TRMNLPaint
            (<%= render DocsRef.new(page: :paint_api) %>).
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Releases and minification') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The repository's release task compiles, minifies, and precompresses (gzip and brotli) every published version reproducibly; those
            artifacts are what the releases CDN serves. If you don't need a modified build, serve a released version
            directly and skip the toolchain entirely.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Minifying your own build is optional. If you do, keep custom properties intact: the framework's JavaScript
            reads public <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">--*</code> variables from the live
            cascade.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <%= render 'framework/page_navigation' %>
  </div>
</div>
